<Step id="STEP_5_VERIFY">
      <Goal>Run the verification gate, then any extra ticket §5 commands. Log only final results.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Run `npx gennady sdd-verify --profile <by kind>` (`AX_PERMITTED_BASH_COMMANDS`) — the ONE
        command this step calls; it fixes what it can and verifies the rest INSIDE itself, so never
        call `npm run fix` / `format:fix` / `lint:fix` yourself first, and never run it a second time
        because the ticket's §5 mentions it (it must not — the table carries only the EXTRA commands
        beyond this gate; a `sdd-verify` row there is a scaffold defect: run the ladder once, log one
        `ver` line for it, and note the duplicate row as a `discovery` line). It walks a fixed ladder,
        cheapest-and-most-important first: `type-check` → `test`/`test:coverage` (**foundation** —
        a failure here HALTS the ladder, nothing later runs: no point polishing code that does not
        build or breaks tests) → `format:fix` → `lint:fix` (mutating repair, marker `🔧` — a failure
        here is a FINDING, never a halt) → `lint` → `format` (read-only, accumulate) → `yagni` (`full`
        only). When the repair rungs actually rewrote files, the foundation (`type-check`, `test`)
        re-runs ONCE over the repaired tree — reported as `(re-run после мутаций)` — so the verdict
        always describes the code you are handing off, never its pre-repair state. Profile by phase
        kind: `bootstrap` / `config` / `doc` → `setup`; `impl` / `refactor` →
        `code` (same ladder as `setup` — type-check · test · format:fix · lint:fix · lint · format;
        tests included since fresh code may have broken existing ones); `test` → `test` (type-check ·
        test:coverage · format:fix · format — no lint/lint:fix, no production code in a test-only
        phase; coverage is measured, its threshold is audit's job, not this gate's); a `fix` phase
        takes the profile of the kind it repairs. `full` (type-check · test:coverage · lint · format ·
        yagni — the only profile with no repair rungs: a final verdict must never mutate the sources
        it judges; `test:coverage` still writes its report into `coverage/`) runs
        once at the end (orchestrator close, right before the group audit dispatch — `AX_AUDIT_HOOK` —
        never here per phase). A gate whose npm script is not declared in `package.json` is skipped
        with an honest `⏭` line — EXCEPT the foundation rungs of `code`/`test`/`full`
        (`type-check` + `test`/`test:coverage`): those are required, and an absent or `echo`-stub
        script there is a red `⛔` verdict, not a skip — a phase must never pass on zero real checks.
        That verdict is not fixable from inside the phase: emit `BLOCKER_FORMAT` naming the missing
        infrastructure and halt `H_BLOCKED`; the infra tickets that build those scripts
        (`sdd-task` → `GATE_QUEUE`) run first. ONE exception, and STEP_1's output declares it: when
        `sdd-task --phase` printed `INFRA_QUEUE_EXEMPTION`, THIS ticket is one of those infra tickets —
        verify with `--profile setup` instead of the profile for your kind (setup has no required
        rungs, so it does not ⛔ on the very scripts this ticket exists to build), log the command you
        actually ran, and add a `discovery` line recording that the phase's verification was partial. Likewise `test:coverage` passing means a FRESH report
        appeared under `coverage/` — a command that exits 0 measuring nothing is reported red.
        If this phase authored production `.ts`/`.tsx`, regardless of phase kind, also run
        `npx gennady lint --spec=<owning module spec> <exact production Target Files>`
        immediately after authoring and again here. Do not pass tool-config or vendor-generated files;
        `export default <expr>` is module wiring and is not a DbC entity.
        — closed-world inventory sync (`ERR_CLI_LINT_INVENTORY_UNDECLARED`) + cast safety
        (`ERR_CLI_LINT_UNSAFE_CAST`). An exported entity not in the inventory is drift
        (`AX_CLOSED_WORLD_INVENTORY`): remove it, or — if genuinely needed — log an `intro <name> ← <reason>`
        line via `npx gennady sdd-log <ticket> line "intro <name> ← <reason>" --phase <PhaseID>`
        (`AX_INTRODUCED_DISCIPLINE`, `AX_TICKET_WRITE_SCOPE`; the worker leaves the spec to audit, `AX_SPEC_NEVER_EDITED` — the intro
        line flags it for inventory backflow at audit). MUST resolve every out-of-inventory export this way. A cast → remove (proper type / guard) or a documented `@ts-expect-error` (`AX_NO_SILENT_ESCAPE_HATCH` — never a silent suppression).
        Run `sdd-verify`, `gennady lint`, and every ticket §5 command SEQUENTIALLY, one at a time, never
        in parallel: every profile that runs `test:coverage` writes `coverage/`, as does
        `gennady testcov --run`, so two of them at once race — whichever finishes last overwrites or
        half-writes the other's report, producing a false red unrelated to the code. (Repair rungs
        rewrite source files besides; a parallel gate would be judging a tree changing under it.)
        Then run each ticket §5 command **verbatim** (the Canonical Gate per `AX_VERIFICATION_BEFORE_HANDOFF`),
        exactly as written — the §5 table holds only the EXTRA commands beyond the phase gate, so
        nothing there repeats `sdd-verify`. **Error ownership scales by write zone, not by age:** for every failure the gate
        surfaces, locate the file the fix must be written into. That file is inside this phase's Target Files,
        or is a file this phase itself created → fix it here, now, regardless of whether the failure pre-dates
        this phase; "pre-existing" is never grounds to skip a fix that lands inside scope. That file sits outside
